  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2025 PLoS ONE article is best understood as a methodological anchor for the field of biological resources: it brings together citation evidence for key resources, including model organisms, and is already documented with 18 RRID-linked resource identifiers. The broader impact record is still early, with 0 OpenAlex citations, but the paper is open access and has already been reused in one Europe PMC article mention.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Do organisms need an impact factor? addresses a practical problem in biomedical resource stewardship: many transgenic animals, antibodies, and related stock-center resources are essential to research, but their use is often difficult to track unless investigators cite them consistently. The article is positioned as a resource-focused, evidence-based analysis rather than a conventional hypothesis paper, and its RRID coverage suggests a strong methodological role in making biological resources more visible and citable.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The record shows clear signs of infrastructure-minded impact. The paper is openly available through multiple open instances, including the journal site, PubMed, and PubMed Central, which supports accessibility for the communities that use and manage these resources. It is also backed by NIH-related funding tied to resource and repository programs, fitting its emphasis on research stewardship and traceability.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the downstream uptake record is still limited. OpenAlex currently reports no citations, and there is no broader public discourse captured in the available event data. That does not diminish the article\u2019s relevance; it instead suggests that its influence is likely to accumulate first through specialized resource-management and methods communities, where citation practice, RRID use, and stock-center attribution matter most.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest evidence of impact therefore lies less in citation volume than in function: the paper contributes a framework for recognizing and measuring the use of core biological resources. With one documented reuse in Europe PMC and a substantial RRID footprint, it appears to be an early-stage reference point for research-resource accounting and for conversations about how organisms and other shared tools are credited in biomedicine.</p>",
